HTR is implemented as described by Liu et al., Nanotechnol. Rev. 2022,
doi:10.1515/ntrev-2022-0044, and extended in HTR+ (Shi et al., J. Phys.: Condens.
Matter 2024, doi:10.1088/1361-648X/ad52df). Its results are checked against the
authors' published program, https://gitee.com/TxxLewis/HTR.

The averaged Steinhardt order parameter follows Lechner & Dellago, J. Chem. Phys.
2008, doi:10.1063/1.2977970; its test reference values come from freud
(BSD-3-Clause, https://github.com/glotzerlab/freud), which is used to generate test
data only. The MCG order parameter follows Barnes, Beckham, Wu & Sum, J. Chem. Phys.
2014, doi:10.1063/1.4871898.

The tetrahedral order parameter q follows Errington & Debenedetti, Nature 2001,
doi:10.1038/35053024, and S_g/S_k follow Chau & Hardwick, Mol. Phys. 1998,
doi:10.1080/002689798169195. Its test reference values in
tests/fixtures/reference/tetra come from GROMACS 2026.2 `gmx order -Sg -Sk`
(LGPL-2.1-or-later, https://www.gromacs.org), which is used to generate test data
only; no GROMACS code is included.

The guest-containing fixtures in tests/fixtures/guests, the crystal fixtures in
tests/fixtures/crystals and the benchmark crystals in tests/fixtures/benchmark were
generated with GenIce2 (MIT; Matsumoto et al., J. Comput. Chem. 2018,
doi:10.1002/jcc.25077).
tests/fixtures/crystals/example_H2O_frame0.gro is the first frame of the example
trajectory distributed with TRACE.

Rust dependency licences are declared by each crate listed in Cargo.toml.
